Chapter: QemuVirtBoardTest

  cites Boards chapter QemuVirtBoard

 Exercise the QEMU virt board module: address constants and UART helper
 functions.

 The four address checks are pure -- comparing a constant touches no
 register, and they stay bound with plain lets. The two putc calls reach
 mmio-write-8, so they carry [Device.Mmio] and are act-bound. That split
 is the whole point of the row: it says which of these six sub-tests goes
 to the bus and which does not.

Section: Definitions

  test-a64-uart-base : Integer
  test-a64-uart-base =
    if qemu-a64-pl011-base == #09000000 then 1 else 0

  test-rv-uart-base : Integer
  test-rv-uart-base =
    if qemu-rv-uart-base == #10000000 then 1 else 0

  test-a64-ram-base : Integer
  test-a64-ram-base =
    if qemu-a64-ram-base == #40000000 then 1 else 0

  test-rv-ram-base : Integer
  test-rv-ram-base =
    if qemu-rv-ram-base == #80000000 then 1 else 0

  test-a64-uart-putc : [Device.Mmio] Integer
  test-a64-uart-putc = act
    w <- (qemu-a64-uart-putc 65)
    1
  end

  test-rv-uart-putc : [Device.Mmio] Integer
  test-rv-uart-putc = act
    w <- (qemu-rv-uart-putc 65)
    1
  end

  opening : [Device.Mmio] Integer = act
    ap <- test-a64-uart-putc
    rp <- test-rv-uart-putc
    test-a64-uart-base + test-rv-uart-base + test-a64-ram-base + test-rv-ram-base + ap + rp
  end

